Title: LOW TIDE
Credit: a two-part radio mystery
Author: the audiodrama demo
Draft date: 2026

====

FADE IN:

EXT. CAUSEWAY - DUSK
[[day:1]]

A stone road across the sand, a mile long, and the sea coming back to cover it. Gulls wheel over wet footprints.

TOBY, twelve, runs the last stretch with his shoes in his hand.

TOBY
(out of breath)
Twenty minutes. She said twenty minutes and it's been nineteen.

Something green knocks against the stones. A bottle, corked, with paper rolled up inside.

He stops. Picks it up. Looks at the water, which is already over his ankles.

TOBY
Nineteen and a half.

He sprints for the shore.

CUT TO:

INT. POST OFFICE - NIGHT
[[day:1]]

One counter, one clock, a thousand pigeonholes. A radio murmurs the shipping forecast.

MAEVE, sixties, sorts letters that do not need sorting. The door bangs open. Toby, soaked to the knee.

MAEVE
You were told twenty minutes.

TOBY
I was nineteen and a half. Look what came in on the tide.

He sets the bottle on the counter. Maeve works the cork out with a letter knife and unrolls the paper.

INSERT - THE LETTER

TO WHOEVER KEEPS THE POST. THE LIGHT GOES ON AT HIGH WATER. BRING STAMPS.

BACK TO SCENE

MAEVE
This is a joke.

TOBY
Who writes a joke and puts it in the sea?

MAEVE
Nobody on this island has a sense of humour. That's why I'm worried.

RADIO (V.O.)
Rockall, Malin. Southwest, gale eight later. Rain. Poor.

She switches the radio off.

TOBY
What light?

MAEVE
There's only one light. And it has been dark for twenty years.

She takes a sheet of stamps from the drawer, looks at it for a long moment, and puts it in her coat.

CUT TO:

EXT. HARBOUR - NIGHT
[[day:1]]

Water slapping at a jetty. A small ferry shifts on its ropes. OWEN, seventies, coils a line with the patience of a man paid by the hour.

OWEN
No.

MAEVE
I haven't asked you anything.

OWEN
You've got your coat on and the boy's got a torch. No.

TOBY
The letter says the light goes on at high water.

OWEN
The light went off when they automated it. Then they un-automated it, and then they forgot it. Nobody crosses to the rock at high water. Not me, not the lifeboat, not the Almighty.

MAEVE
Then we'll walk out at low water and wait.

OWEN
(beat)
There's a tin of biscuits under my seat. Take it. If you're going to be stupid, don't be hungry.

EXT. LIGHTHOUSE ROCK - NIGHT
[[day:1]]

Wind. Surf on three sides. The lighthouse above them, black against black.

Maeve and Toby climb the last steps with the biscuit tin and a torch.

TOBY
It's locked.

MAEVE
Of course it's locked. It's a lighthouse, not a bus shelter.

Waves boom against the rock below. The sea is rising around them, and the way back has already gone under.

TOBY
Auntie Maeve. The causeway.

MAEVE
I know.

TOBY
We can't get back until morning.

MAEVE
I know, Toby.

Far above them, a click. Then a hum, climbing. Then light: a white beam swings out over the sea for the first time in twenty years.

The door unbolts from the inside and swings open. An old woman in a cardigan stands there holding a candle she no longer needs.

IRIS
You took your time. Did you bring the stamps?

FADE TO BLACK.
